= Biology bibliography

= Clinician Brief: Arsenic by ATSDR
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.atsdr.cdc.gov/environmental-medicine/hcp/clinician-briefs/arsenic.html

Patient management: acute exposure, supportive care and specialist consultation concerning chelation.

= Pepto-Bismol by the NHS
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/pepto-bismol/

Black stools and tongue with bismuth subsalicylate; this modern formulation is not equated with an 1864 recipe.

= Bleeding from the bottom by the NHS
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/bleeding-from-the-bottom-rectal-bleeding/

Dark stools: oral iron and differential interpretation.

= Senna by the NHS
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/senna/

Side effects: yellow-brown or red-brown urine.

= Digoxin by MedlinePlus
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://medlineplus.gov/druginfo/meds/a682301.html

Why is this medication prescribed?: modern cardiac indications.

= The British Pharmacopoeia in Chemical News (1864)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1864}

https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/The_chemical_news._Volume_9%2C_January_-_June_1864._%28IA_s713id13683310%29.pdf

Volume 9, contemporary serial discussion, especially 6 February, p. 65, iron preparations. No personal byline is established for the cited notice.

= A Manual of Practical Therapeutics by Edward John Waring (1865)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1865}
{tag=Work by Edward John Waring}

https://chestofbooks.com/health/materia-medica-drugs/Practical-Therapeutics/index.html

Second edition, John Churchill and Sons, 1865. Used for historical observations and the wet hydrated-peroxide procedure, not as authority to import later pharmacopoeial names.

= The Prescriber's Analysis of the British Pharmacopoeia by John Birkbeck Nevins (1864)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1864}
{tag=Work by John Birkbeck Nevins}

https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/The_prescriber's_analysis_of_the_British_Pharmacopoeia_(electronic_resource)_(IA_b20386874).pdf

Contemporary first-edition commentary, identified by https://wellcomecollection.org/works/fusrerqt[Wellcome's 1864 catalogue record]. Printed pages are three less than the zero-based PDF page index. Strengths and terminology are specific to 1864; some abbreviated numerical entries disagree with the detailed monographs and are not silently reconciled.

= Antepartum haemorrhage by RCOG
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=Green-top Guideline 63}

https://www.rcog.org.uk/media/pwdi1tef/gtg_63.pdf

Section 7.3: avoid digital vaginal examination when placenta praevia remains possible until ultrasound excludes it. This is a professional source for the specific examination precaution.

= Quarterly Report on Midwifery (April 1864)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1864}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183815/

April 1864, pp. 549–556. Period discussion of uterine disease and obstetric complications; used as contemporary evidence, not current treatment guidance.

= Diseases of the Urinary Organs (1842)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1842}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5097492/

Contemporary clinical text discussing retention, opium and the warm bath. Historical recommendations are distinguished from current catheter care.

= Gonococcal infections among adolescents and adults by CDC
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cdc.gov/std/treatment-guidelines/gonorrhea-adults.htm

Current STI treatment guidance: ceftriaxone-based treatment, microbiological testing and management of partners.

= Digoxin tablets summary of product characteristics (emc)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/102583/smpc

Sections 4.1–4.5: indications, contraindications, renal function, electrolyte disturbances and interactions.

= Aloes folii succus siccatus by the European Medicines Agency
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/herbal/aloes-folii-succus-siccatus

https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/herbal-summary/aloes-summary-public_en.pdf[Current public summary: short-term constipation use and contraindications].

= Indwelling catheterisation in adults by EAUN (2024)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://nurses.uroweb.org/wp-content/uploads/EAUN-Guideline-indwelling-catheterisation-2024.pdf

Especially §§4.3–4.6: urethral catheter contraindications and indications and contraindications for suprapubic catheterisation.

= Lower urinary tract symptoms in men by NICE
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg97/chapter/recommendations

CG97, §1.7: immediate catheterisation for acute urinary retention.

= Placenta praevia, placenta accreta and vasa praevia by RCOG
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.rcog.org.uk/for-the-public/browse-our-patient-information/placenta-praevia-placenta-accreta-and-vasa-praevia/

Placental location, ultrasound, bleeding and delivery planning.

= Hypertension in pregnancy by NICE
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng133/chapter/recommendations

NG133, especially §§1.8.1–1.8.6 and 1.8.13: magnesium sulfate, severe hypertension and mode of birth.

= Pneumonia: diagnosis and management by NICE
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng250/chapter/recommendations

NG250, 2025, especially diagnosis, severity assessment, antibiotics and respiratory support.

= Report of a Case of Puerperal Convulsions (1864)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1864}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9423387/

June 1864, pp. 411–413. A historical case report illustrating bloodletting, chloroform and the failure of delivery alone to guarantee cessation of convulsions. Its causal theory and remedies are not current guidance.

= Royal Infirmary surgical ward report (1863–1864)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=published 1865}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5313652/

Report of Clinical Cases in the Surgical Wards of the Royal Infirmary, July 1863 to October 1864. The report was printed after the examination; p. 111 describes practice during the period, including wound care and the use of quinine and stimulants in pyaemia.

= Treatment of acute rheumatism in the Edinburgh Medical Journal (1862)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1862}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5277574/

Practice of Medicine, pp. 179–183: contemporary reports of alkaline treatment and discussion of the limitations of comparisons between cases.

= Practical Notes on Diagnosis, Prognosis, and Treatment in Cases of Delirium Tremens by Thomas Laycock
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1862}
{tag=Work by Thomas Laycock}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5277486/

Contemporary clinical discussion, including the medicinal-treatment discussion on pp. 411–413. Historical evidence of disagreement over opium, stimulation and prognosis.

= Urinary Deposits (review, 1853)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1853}
{tag=Work by Arthur Hill Hassall}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5186183/

<Arthur Hill Hassall>'s contemporary review of the fourth edition of Urinary Deposits. Bibliographic identity: British and Foreign Medico-Chirurgical Review 12(23), July 1853, pp. 123–149. Printed pp. 128–129 discuss the value of microscopy even when no visible deposit is present. The recommendation elsewhere in the review to retain urine for days is not adopted as current sample-handling practice.

= Urinalysis in Clinical Methods by David M. Roxe
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1990}
{tag=Work by David M. Roxe}

https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK302/

Chapter 191 of Clinical Methods, third edition. Definition, Technique, Basic Science and Clinical Significance. The physical, chemical and microscopic framework is used with modern reagent-strip methods explicitly distinguished from the 1864 examination.

= Nuisances Removal Act for England (Amendment) Act 1863
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=26 & 27 Vict. c.117}

https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/26-27/117/pdfs/ukpga_18630117_en.pdf

Original printed Act, enacted 28 July 1863. Section 2 provides inspection, seizure and a justice's disposal order for unfit food; it does not provide an exhaustive disease-by-disease whole-carcass list.

= On Poisoning by Diseased Pork by Julius Althaus
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Julius Althaus}
{title2=1864}

https://wellcomecollection.org/works/dm2gtp39

London: John Churchill and Sons, 1864, 34 pages. Full title continues “being an essay on trichinosis, or flesh-worm disease, its prevention and cure”. Original scan: https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/On_poisoning_by_diseased_pork_-_being_an_essay_on_trichinosis%2C_or_flesh-worm_disease%2C_its_prevention_and_cure_%28IA_b22267372%29.pdf. The opening discussion connects pork, trichinae and microscopic examination; the historical remedies are not present treatment advice.

= Current United States meat-inspection regulations, 9 CFR Part 311
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-9/chapter-III/subchapter-A/part-311

Present United States comparative criteria, specifically https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-9/chapter-III/subchapter-A/part-311/section-311.2[§311.2, tuberculosis] and https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-9/chapter-III/subchapter-A/part-311/section-311.16[§311.16, carcasses presenting a food-poisoning risk]. These are not English law in 1864.

= About Anthrax by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.cdc.gov/anthrax/about/

Present institutional account of infection through animal products and contaminated meat.

= Lead in Drinking Water by the Drinking Water Inspectorate
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.dwi.gov.uk/lead-in-drinking-water/

“Where does lead come from?” and “How does lead get into drinking water?”: plumbing, stagnation, temperature, <water hardness>, disturbance and intermittent particles.

= Pipe and Fittings Corrosion by the Drinking Water Inspectorate
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.dwi.gov.uk/pipe-and-fittings-corrosion-2/

Present institutional explanation of <lead> solubility, <pH>, <alkalinity> and deposits; used for chemistry, not a period legal limit.

= Case of Criminal Poisoning with Oxalic Acid, in Which Perforation of the Stomach Took Place by Henry Duncan Littlejohn
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Henry Duncan Littlejohn}
{title2=1861}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5279526/

Edinburgh Medical Journal 7(1), July 1861, pp.13–20. Original pre-examination report of severe gastric injury and perforation; a particular case, not an invariant post-mortem pattern.

= Acute Renal Failure Following Oxalic Acid Poisoning: A Case Report
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2012}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3527234/

Original case report; renal biopsy showed tubular injury with refractile <calcium oxalate> crystals. The case does not establish that every exposure produces <hypocalcaemia>. Indexed full text was available; direct retrieval intermittently displayed a browser-verification page.

= Plasma Oxalic Acid and Calcium Levels in Oxalate Poisoning
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1967}

https://jcp.bmj.com/content/20/3/283

Journal of Clinical Pathology 20(3), pp.283–285. Original investigation of five patients following <oxalic acid> or ethylene-glycol poisoning; primary indexed abstract: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/5602563/. Used for later biochemical evidence, not attributed to 1864.

= Oxalic Acid: International Chemical Safety Card 0529
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=November 2009}

https://www.inchem.org/documents/icsc/icsc/eics0529.htm

International Chemical Safety Card: corrosive ingestion, acute symptoms and possible effects on <calcium> balance.

= Anticholinergic Syndrome by the Royal Children’s Hospital Melbourne
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Anticholinergic_Syndrome/

Clinical practice guideline, “Background” and “History and Physical Examination”: <muscarinic receptor> antagonism and central/peripheral signs. Used for present scientific interpretation of <belladonna> toxicity.

= Head injury: assessment and early management by NICE
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=NG232}

https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/NG232/chapter/recommendations

Recommendations §§1.4.1–1.4.2 distinguish initial stabilization from assessment and require exclusion of important <traumatic brain injury> before attributing impaired <consciousness> to intoxication. Indexed official text was available; direct retrieval intermittently returned HTTP 403.

= Head injury and concussion by the National Health Service
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/head-injury-and-concussion/

Institutional description of <head injury>, <concussion> and warning signs; present medical comparison.

= Alcohol poisoning by the National Health Service
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/alcohol-poisoning/

Institutional description of symptoms, including severe disturbance of <consciousness> and <breathing>; used for present medical comparison.

= Prone and Postural Respiration in Drowning, and Other Forms of Apnoea (review, 1858)
{c}
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{title2=1858}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5186098/

Contemporary anonymous review in The British and Foreign Medico-Chirurgical Review, volume 21, pp. 285–297, of the 1857 work by <Marshall Hall>. The review documents historical terminology and experimental concerns; its postural method is not current resuscitation guidance.

= On Excretine by William Marcet
{c}
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{title2=1859}
{tag=Work by William Marcet}

https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Archives_of_medicine_-_a_record_of_practical_observations_and_anatomical_and_chemical_researches_connected_with_the_investigation_and_treatment_of_disease._Volume_1%2C_1859._%28IA_s2765id1396781%29.pdf

Archives of Medicine, volume 1, pp. 100–102. Contemporary account of extraction, solubility, melting behaviour and equivalent-weight analysis. The early analytical claims must be distinguished from later revisions of purity and identity.

