{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=2021}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9364424/

AHA scientific statement, 2021. The Free Wall Rupture section supports the present-day comparison: friable infarct tissue, collapse, haemopericardium, tamponade and echocardiographic diagnosis. It is not used as evidence of technology available in 1864.

= On Fatty Diseases of the Heart by Richard Quain
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Richard Quain}
{title2=1850 account; 1852 reproduction}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5807567/

An 1852 reproduction of the 1850 Medico-Chirurgical Transactions account. Edinburgh Medical and Surgical Journal 77(190), pp. 120–152. Pages 120–126 distinguish external fatty growth from degeneration within muscular fibres and discuss microscopic appearances and specimen selection. Historical theories about transformation into fat are not adopted as present-day explanations.

= Gallstone ileus with rupture of the heart by J. B. Junor
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by J. B. Junor}
{title2=1858}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5311768/

Original article titled Notes of a Case of Ileus, Caused by a Large Gall-Stone Impacted in the Ileum; Death from Rupture of the Heart. Edinburgh Medical Journal 3(11), May 1858, pp. 1004–1005. The report describes blood in the pericardium and an extremely friable, fatty right ventricular wall.

Bibliographic verification: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29646367/

