https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/cupping

Evidence and Safety: low quality of much efficacy research, and harms including <burns>, <scars>, <infection> and <anaemia> from repeated <wet cupping>. Used to qualify the ancient absolute claim of harmlessness.

= On the Treatment of Acute Diseases by Aretaeus of Cappadocia
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=Ancient work; digitized 1856 edition}

https://beta.perseus.tufts.edu/urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0719.tlg003.perseus-grc2:2/

Primary Greek and accompanying historical English text. Book II.3, treatment of cardiac affections: impending collapse, the rationale for selective depletion, and the assigned sequence of <venesection>, <cupping>, <wine>, <poultices> and <enemas>. Distinguish this work from the existing Causes and Signs of Chronic Diseases.

= Health Tract No.154: Disinfectants (1865)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1865}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9199822/

1865, volume 12, p.154. Primary tract on cleanliness, deodorization and proposed disinfectants. Its recipes, miasma explanations and oxygen-refreshing claims are not endorsed.

= Notes on Nursing by Florence Nightingale
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1860}
{tag=Work by Florence Nightingale}

https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/nightingale/nursing/nursing.html

Pre-1865 work, institutional digital transcription. “Ventilation and Warming”, “Fumigations”: remove the source rather than relying on fumigating fluids. No inaccessible later edition is substituted for a historical chemical recipe.

= Water, Sanitation and Health in Humanitarian Emergencies by the World Health Organization
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://www.who.int/teams/environment-climate-change-and-health/water-sanitation-and-health/environmental-health-in-emergencies/humanitarian-emergencies

Institutional explanation of epidemic risks, enteric disease, contact exposures and mosquito breeding. Cited to distinguish exposure routes, not to prescribe a historical water allowance.

= Iodine by the National Institutes of Health Office of Dietary Supplements
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}

https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iodine-HealthProfessional/

Health Professional Fact Sheet, “Iodine deficiency”: goitre and neurodevelopmental consequences, distinguished from historical lime-water claims.

= The British Soldier in India in The British and Foreign Medico-Chirurgical Review (1861)
{parent=Biology bibliography}
{c}
{title2=1861}

https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5182393/

January 1861, 27(53), pp.38–48; especially pp.43–46, an asserted lime-water explanation for goitre and cretinism. This primary period review documents the historical belief, not a correct modern causal conclusion. PMC metadata misspells “Soldier” as “Solider”.

